// 06 · refinement — the constructor is the proof
//
// A refinement type carries a predicate. A value typed `Level` below is not
// just an Int — it is an Int for which `0 <= self <= 100` holds. There is no
// separate validation phase and no defensive guard downstream: the invariant
// holds by the time the value exists, because building the value IS the proof
// obligation.
//
// Where the medium can decide the predicate from constants, it discharges it at
// compile time and leaves no runtime check behind. Where the arguments are
// dynamic, it keeps the check at the boundary. Either way, a value that would
// violate its type cannot be constructed — the medium refuses.
//
// Run it:   mentl run  lib/tutorial/06-refinement.mn   ← prints 51
// Read it:  mentl lib/tutorial/06-refinement.mn:19:6   ← the cursor on Level; the refinement in the ring
import io

// Level — an Int narrowed by a predicate. `self` is the value being refined.
type Level = Int where 0 <= self && self <= 100

// Construction is decidable here: 50 satisfies 0 <= 50 <= 100, so Verify
// discharges it from the constant and no check survives to runtime.
fn half() -> Level = 50

// Uncomment to watch the medium refuse it. 250 > 100 is decidably false, so
// this line never compiles — E_RefinementRejected at the value, not a warning
// downstream. The illegal value has no way to exist.
// fn over() -> Level = 250
//
// brighten takes a Level and needs no `if lvl < 0` guard: the bound is part of
// the type, already proven before lvl arrives. The compiler erased the check.
fn brighten(lvl: Level) = lvl + 1

fn main() = brighten(half()) |> int_to_str |> print_string
